<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
    <!-- The app ships outside the Mac App Store, signed with Developer ID and notarized, so it is
         deliberately NOT sandboxed. See adr/0002-app-distribution-and-updates.md (4).

         com.apple.security.app-sandbox was removed because Velopack cannot live with it: the updater
         replaces the whole .app bundle in place and asks for elevation through AppleScript, both of
         which the sandbox forbids (https://docs.velopack.io/packaging/operating-systems/macos).
         Three entitlements went with it, all of which only mean anything under the sandbox:
           - network.client, required there to open outgoing connections
           - files.user-selected.read-only and files.downloads.read-only
           - assets.{pictures,music,movies}.read-only
         The last five existed because the file picker silently refused to become visible under the
         sandbox without them. Unsandboxed the app reaches the user's files without asking. -->
    <dict>
        <!-- TODO when the app is signed with a real Apple Developer provisioning profile: add
                 <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
                 <array><string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier)</string></array>
             so SecureStorage stores the device token in the keychain. It must NOT be added for
             ad-hoc-signed local builds — macOS SIGKILLs the app at launch for requesting an
             unprovisioned entitlement. Until then MauiSecureTokenStore's fallback applies. -->
    </dict>
</plist>
